          "recommended_because": "The zero is true, and the footnote that defines it is where the commitment actually lives.",
          "text": "Macquarie said it would run off its on-balance sheet lending and equity exposures to coal companies by the end of 2024, and on 31 December 2024 it reported zero. The number is not in dispute. What it counts is.\n\nFootnote 29, attached to the commitment sentence, removes four things from the measure before it is taken: coal-company securities held on the balance sheet for client facilitation and market-making; leasing and asset finance; short-term financing that generates trading income — inventory finance, pre-payments, receivables finance, letters of credit; and issued guarantees not tied to a future loan, which the footnote says includes mine rehabilitation bonds. Every one of those is real money at risk to a real coal company. None of them appears in the zero.\n\nThen there is the word “run-off”. A run-off is not an exit — it is writing no new business and letting existing facilities mature on their own terms. Between 2021 and the end of 2024 there are three years, and most corporate lending is shorter than that. Macquarie has never published what its coal exposure was in 2021, so there is no way from outside to tell a deliberate reduction from a portfolio that simply ran to term.\n\nThis is not an accusation of bad faith. The definition is published, the carve-outs are printed, and the bank did not have to disclose any of it. But a commitment measured in units the same footnote defines is a commitment about a definition, and it should be read as one."
